{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc | sed -n '155,330p'; rg -n 'packet-call-provenance|packet->projection|packet->vector|call-provenance|image-hash|register-hash' src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/*.cljc src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn*code.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"   155\t   :register \"DebruijnRegisterVM\"})\n   156\t\n   157\t\n   158\t(defn- packet->projection\n   159\t  \"The full row-lane projection of one expanded tree packet — vector,\n   160\t   address, and provenance side tables together (cc3 item 3: this\n   161\t   choke point used to select `(:vector …)` and discard the rest).\"\n   162\t  [packet]\n   163\t  (linearize/project-rows (macro/packet->row-set packet)))\n   164\t\n   165\t\n   166\t(defn- packet->vector\n   167\t  [packet]\n   168\t  (:vector (packet->projection packet)))\n   169\t\n   170\t\n   171\t(defn packet-call-provenance\n   172\t  \"The call provenance of one expanded tree `packet` in session\n   173\t   `state`, resolved from the expander's retained C1 side tables\n   174\t   (`call-site-tables`, keyed `[admission-origin final-root]`):\n   175\t   `{:semantic [[segment pc origin root path] …] :stack […] :register\n   176\t   […]}` over the same images the loaders derive, or nil when the\n   177\t   packet's root claimed no C1 (absence, never invention). Two\n   178\t   retained admissions for one root refuse with\n   179\t   `:rule :ambiguous-call-site-table` — a table is never borrowed\n   180\t   across admissions. Segment coordinates are the images' own\n   181\t   content identities, as the loaders compute them.\"\n   182\t  [state packet]\n   183\t  (let [root (first packet)\n   184\t        tables (:call-site-tables (:expander state))\n   185\t        matches (into []\n   186\t                      (filter (fn [[[_origin r] _rows]] (= r root)))\n   187\t                      tables)]\n   188\t    (cond\n   189\t      (empty? matches) nil\n   190\t      (< 1 (count matches))\n   191\t      (throw (ex-info \"Two retained admissions claim one packet root\"\n   192\t                      {:rule :ambiguous-call-site-table, :root root}))\n   193\t      :else\n   194\t      (let [rows (vec (second (first matches)))\n   195\t            proj (linearize/project-rows (macro/packet->row-set packet)\n   196\t                                         nil rows)\n   197\t            v (:vector proj)\n   198\t            sem-address (ucf/code-address v)\n   199\t            ;; each image is derived ONCE, provenance attached: the\n   200\t            ;; segment coordinate is the image own content identity\n   201\t            ;; (unchanged by provenance), patched into the rows as the\n   202\t            ;; loaders compute it\n   203\t            stack (derive/stack-image\n   204\t                    v {:call-provenance (:call-provenance proj)})\n   205\t            stack-address (dcode/image-hash (:image stack))\n   206\t            register (derive/register-image\n   207\t                       v {:call-provenance (:call-provenance proj)})\n   208\t            register-address (rcode/register-hash register)]\n   209\t        {:semantic (mapv (fn [[pc o r p]] [sem-address pc o r p])\n   210\t                         (:call-provenance proj))\n   211\t         :stack (mapv (fn [row] (assoc row 0 stack-address))\n   212\t                      (:call-provenance stack))\n   213\t         :register (mapv (fn [row] (assoc row 0 register-address))\n   214\t                         (:call-provenance register))}))))\n   215\t\n   216\t\n   217\t(defn- append-stack-image\n   218\t  \"Attach `image` after the stack image `vm` already holds and start at\n   219\t   its first instruction.  A closure a previous input stored (a `def`)\n   220\t   names a body pc in the earlier image, so that image is kept, not\n   221\t   replaced.  `stack/attach-image` admits the incoming image alone under\n   222\t   the current stamp (the expander and the lowerer are its fresh\n   223\t   producers), relocates and appends it, records its offset-table row,\n   224\t   and keeps `:hash` the canonical H of the whole `:segment`\n   225\t   (yin.vm.linker.md section 7.3).  Attaching touches no register, so\n   226\t   starting the input is this function's: `:pc` at the image's row, the\n   227\t   registers a fresh run starts with.  An input whose image is already\n   228\t   held reruns at that row.\"\n   229\t  [vm image]\n   230\t  (let [attached (stack/attach-image vm image vm/stack-contract)]\n   231\t    (assoc attached\n   232\t           :pc (stack/absolute-pc attached [(dcode/image-hash image) 0])\n   233\t           :frames []\n   234\t           :stack []\n   235\t           :continuation []\n   236\t           :halted? false\n   237\t           :blocked? false\n   238\t           :value nil)))\n   239\t\n   240\t\n   241\t(defn- append-register-image\n   242\t  \"The register image counterpart of `append-stack-image`.  The image it\n   243\t   runs in is the concatenation, whose later main bodies end in `:halt` as\n   244\t   the first does, and `:hash` is the canonical R of that concatenation.\n   245\t   Starting the input sizes the register file for the main body at the\n   246\t   image's row.\"\n   247\t  [vm image]\n   248\t  (let [attached (register/attach-image vm image vm/register-contract)\n   249\t        pc (register/absolute-pc attached [(rcode/register-hash image) 0])\n   250\t        body (some #(when (= pc (:start %)) %)\n   251\t                   (:bodies (:segment attached)))]\n   252\t    (assoc attached\n   253\t           :pc pc\n   254\t           :frames []\n   255\t           :registers (vec (repeat (or (:registers body) 0) nil))\n   256\t           :continuation []\n   257\t           :halted? false\n   258\t           :blocked? false\n   259\t           :value nil)))\n   260\t\n   261\t\n   262\t(def program-loaders\n   263\t  \"The loader the evaluator observer hands each `program-out` value, per\n   264\t   evaluator.  Every value there is one expanded canonical tree packet\n   265\t   `[root rows]` (yin.vm.macro.md §2.4); each loader takes its row set — the\n   266\t   ast-walker through `vm-load-rows`, the semantic VM through\n   267\t   `semantic/load-vector` over the v4 projection, and the de Bruijn\n   268\t   kernels through A-derived stack and register images.\n   269\t   No evaluator learns that an expander ran.\"\n   270\t  {:ast-walker (fn [vm packet]\n   271\t                 (ast-walker/vm-load-rows vm\n   272\t                                          (macro/packet->row-set packet)\n   273\t                                          vm/ast-contract))\n   274\t   ;; every loader here is the trusted fresh-producer path: the only\n   275\t   ;; producer of `program-out` is the expander, so each supplies the\n   276\t   ;; current stamp itself\n   277\t   :semantic (fn [vm packet]\n   278\t               (semantic/load-vector vm (packet->vector packet)\n   279\t                                     vm/semantic-contract))\n   280\t   :stack (fn [vm packet]\n   281\t            (append-stack-image\n   282\t              vm\n   283\t              (:image (derive/stack-image (packet->vector packet)))))\n   284\t   :register (fn [vm packet]\n   285\t               (append-register-image\n   286\t                 vm\n   287\t                 (derive/register-image (packet->vector packet))))})\n   288\t\n   289\t\n   290\t(def command-heads\n   291\t  \"Commands this shell answers.  `connect` and `disconnect` belong to the\n   292\t   driver, which owns the RPC client; `telemetry` is answered only to say that\n   293\t   this REPL slice does not have it.\"\n   294\t  #{'vm 'lang 'compile 'reset 'help 'repl-state 'quit 'telemetry 'abandon\n   295\t    'trust 'untrust 'trusted 'host})\n   296\t\n   297\t\n   298\t(def help-text\n   299\t  (str \"Commands:\\n\"\n   300\t       \"  (vm :ast-walker | :semantic | :stack | :register)\\n\"\n   301\t       \"  (lang :yang/clojure | :yang/python | :yang/php)\\n\"\n   302\t       \"  (compile expr)\\n\"\n   303\t       \"  (reset)\\n\"\n   304\t       \"  (trust \\\"ed25519:<64 hex>\\\") / (untrust \\\"ed25519:<64 hex>\\\")\\n\"\n   305\t       \"  (trusted)  - own principal and trusted set (--dht-trust)\\n\"\n   306\t       \"  (connect \\\"daostream:ws://host:port\\\")\\n\"\n   307\t       \"  (disconnect)\\n\"\n   308\t       \"  (require (quote m))  - link module m; with a dht:<dir> index store\"\n   309\t       \" it may fetch m's code from peers, and stays pending until it has\\n\"\n   310\t       \"  (abandon)  - give up a (require ...) that is still pending\\n\"\n   311\t       \"  (require (quote yin.link)), then (yin.link/publish (quote m)\"\n   312\t       \" (quote [f]))  - publish module m exporting f from this session's\"\n   313\t       \" indexed code under the --dht-key principal, and (yin.link/names)\"\n   314\t       \"  - the names this node resolves\\n\"\n   315\t       \"  (require (quote yin.head)), then (yin.head/heads)  - each followed\"\n   316\t       \" principal's installed head, and (yin.head/moved)  - the linked\"\n   317\t       \" names a newer head moved\\n\"\n   318\t       \"  (host)  - the runtime this REPL runs on\\n\"\n   319\t       \"  (repl-state)\\n\"\n   320\t       \"  (help)\\n\"\n   321\t       \"  (quit)\\n\"\n   322\t       \"  *1, *2, *3  - last, second-to-last, and third-to-last evaluated\"\n   323\t       \" values\"))\n   324\t\n   325\t\n   326\t(def host-text\n   327\t  \"One line naming the runtime: JVM and Node carry their version, Dart only\n   328\t   the platform (cljd exposes no version without a dart:io dependency).\n   329\t   The cljs branch must stay load-safe in a browser — the demo build links\n   330\t   this namespace and has no `process` global — so it probes `js/process`\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:158:(defn- packet->projection\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:166:(defn- packet->vector\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:168:  (:vector (packet->projection packet)))\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:171:(defn packet-call-provenance\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:204:                    v {:call-provenance (:call-provenance proj)})\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:205:            stack-address (dcode/image-hash (:image stack))\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:207:                       v {:call-provenance (:call-provenance proj)})\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:208:            register-address (rcode/register-hash register)]\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:210:                         (:call-provenance proj))\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:212:                      (:call-provenance stack))\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:214:                         (:call-provenance register))}))))\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:232:           :pc (stack/absolute-pc attached [(dcode/image-hash image) 0])\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:249:        pc (register/absolute-pc attached [(rcode/register-hash image) 0])\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:278:               (semantic/load-vector vm (packet->vector packet)\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:283:              (:image (derive/stack-image (packet->vector packet)))))\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:287:                 (derive/register-image (packet->vector packet))))})\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_code.cljc:263:(defn register-hash\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:337:                                       :call-provenance (:call-provenance sem-proj)}))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:342:                                          :call-provenance (:call-provenance sem-proj)}))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:344:                      (dissoc register-full :call-provenance)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:346:            h (dcode/image-hash h-image)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:347:            r (rcode/register-hash r-image)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:400:                                                   (:call-provenance sem-proj))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:404:                                                   (:call-provenance stack-full))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:408:                                                   (:call-provenance register-full))})\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc:6:   one function that computes H (`image-hash`), and the validator that is\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc:29:   B1 does not build a decode/receive path: `image-hash`, `encode-image`,\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc:120:   guards every real shape change. `image-hash`'s own docstring carries\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc:449:;; The canonical positional instruction vector's bytes, and image-hash\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc:517:(defn image-hash\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc | sed -n '495,545p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_code.cljc | sed -n '230,280p'; rg -n 'segment-key|defn materialize\"'!'\"' src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc; rg -n 'index|offset|image|:hash|segment' src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/interpreter.cljc | head -65; rg -n '2.4|2.5|segment-address|provenance' collab/yang-clojure/1794670000000-architect-callsite-generalization-design.gpt-6-astra.findings.md | head -35\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc | sed -n '495,545p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_code.cljc | sed -n '230,280p'; rg -n 'segment-key|defn materialize\"'!'\"' src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc; rg -n 'index|offset|image|:hash|segment' src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/interpreter.cljc | head -65; rg -n '2.4|2.5|segment-address|provenance' collab/yang-clojure/1794670000000-architect-callsite-generalization-design.gpt-6-astra.findings.md | head -35\"","aggregated_output":"   495\t    (apply str (get mnemonic-tag (mnemonic-of t))\n   496\t           (map-indexed (fn [i kind] (encode-operand kind (nth t (inc i)))) kinds))))\n   497\t\n   498\t\n   499\t(defn encode-image\n   500\t  \"The canonical positional instruction vector's own bytes (S2 item 6):\n   501\t   every pc-indexed tuple's bytes, in pc order, concatenated with no\n   502\t   outer header -- pc order and each tuple's fixed arity are the only\n   503\t   framing the whole vector needs.\"\n   504\t  [v]\n   505\t  (apply str (map encode-tuple v)))\n   506\t\n   507\t\n   508\t(def descriptor-hash\n   509\t  \"The descriptor's own content hash, under this dimension's own\n   510\t   encoder -- the same 'hash the descriptor through its own canonical\n   511\t   encoder' shape `yin.vm.debruijn/dimension-hash` uses for its\n   512\t   descriptor, computed here with this dimension's distinct encoder\n   513\t   rather than that one.\"\n   514\t  (jing/blake3 (encode-scalar descriptor)))\n   515\t\n   516\t\n   517\t(defn image-hash\n   518\t  \"The ONE function that computes H (S2 item 6): `jing/blake3` over the\n   519\t   descriptor hash (which already folds in the lowering-contract\n   520\t   version) concatenated with the canonical instruction vector's own\n   521\t   bytes -- pc-indexed tuples, refs already resolved to pcs, no header,\n   522\t   exact scalar bytes. `jing/blake3`, not `jing/blake3-bytes`: this\n   523\t   dimension's canonical encoding is hex text (`encode-image` returns a\n   524\t   string, via `framed`/`to-hex`), the same shape `jing/blake3` hashes\n   525\t   for `yin.vm.debruijn/dimension-hash`, so hashing the string directly\n   526\t   is the fit; `blake3-bytes` would apply to a raw byte buffer this\n   527\t   encoder never produces. H is a derived checksum of this backend image;\n   528\t   A is the sole code identity.\"\n   529\t  [v]\n   530\t  (jing/blake3 (str descriptor-hash (encode-image v))))\n   531\t\n   532\t\n   533\t;; =============================================================================\n   534\t;; Host-boundary scalar-class support (S2 item 5)\n   535\t;; =============================================================================\n   536\t\n   537\t(defn image-scalar-classes\n   538\t  \"Every scalar class this image uses, scanned from every :data-kind\n   539\t   operand across every mnemonic -- not only :const's value, but also\n   540\t   :store-get's and :store-put's :data operands, since those are hashed\n   541\t   bytes too and a scalar class hiding only there would let S2's host-\n   542\t   support scan (below) miss it. S2's own wording names :const alone;\n   543\t   scanning every :data operand is strictly more conservative than that\n   544\t   floor, never less, so it does not weaken the design's own rule.\"\n   545\t  [v]\n   230\t  [t]\n   231\t  (let [kinds (tuple-kinds t)]\n   232\t    (apply str\n   233\t           (debruijn-code/encode-scalar (mnemonic-of t))\n   234\t           (map-indexed\n   235\t             (fn [i _kind]\n   236\t               (debruijn-code/encode-scalar (nth t (inc i))))\n   237\t             kinds))))\n   238\t\n   239\t\n   240\t(defn encode-instructions\n   241\t  [instructions]\n   242\t  (apply str (map encode-tuple instructions)))\n   243\t\n   244\t\n   245\t(defn- encode-body\n   246\t  [{:keys [locals registers start end]}]\n   247\t  (debruijn-code/encode-scalar [locals registers start end]))\n   248\t\n   249\t\n   250\t(defn encode-register-image\n   251\t  \"The canonical register image's own bytes (design section 3): every\n   252\t   declared body's `[locals registers start end]` in body order, then\n   253\t   every pc-indexed tuple's bytes in pc order -- the two parts design\n   254\t   section 3's table names ('body ranges plus positional instructions').\"\n   255\t  [{:keys [bodies instructions]}]\n   256\t  (str (apply str (map encode-body bodies)) (encode-instructions instructions)))\n   257\t\n   258\t\n   259\t(def descriptor-hash\n   260\t  (jing/blake3 (debruijn-code/encode-scalar descriptor)))\n   261\t\n   262\t\n   263\t(defn register-hash\n   264\t  \"The ONE function that computes R (design section 3): `R = blake3\n   265\t   (register-descriptor-hash || canonical-register-vector)`. Received\n   266\t   register bytes must be hashed before decoding, as B1's wire rule\n   267\t   requires -- no receive path exists in this phase, but this function's\n   268\t   own shape (bytes in, hash out, no host-value peeking) already supports\n   269\t   that later use. R is a derived backend checksum; A is the sole code\n   270\t   identity.\"\n   271\t  [register-image]\n   272\t  (jing/blake3 (str descriptor-hash (encode-register-image register-image))))\n   273\t\n   274\t\n   275\t;; =============================================================================\n   276\t;; body-liveness (design section 4.5)\n   277\t;; =============================================================================\n   278\t;; A standard backward dataflow, one pure function of the instruction\n   279\t;; vector and the body ranges: per pc, use/def from the section 4.4\n   280\t;; table, successors from the control-flow rule (a tail `:call` has none,\n92:  \"The default algorithm used for implicit minting (content-hash, segment-key,\n199:(defn segment-key\n205:   (segment-key v {:algorithm default-hash-algorithm}))\n389:(defn materialize!\n12:   image over the content pair, appending the response the requesting\n170:   index datoms the source has loaded (none for a name-env source), and\n190:    (throw (ex-info (str \"a dht:<dir> index store is the link source; \"\n301:  \"Link one resolved name to its manifest image, or `::pending`.  The\n318:                :image {:value (:value res)}\n350:                        :image {:value (:value res)}\n467:   decision that refuses is forgotten likewise (a later index load may\n1:# Call-site provenance: occurrence contract and durable correspondence\n12:Adopt one language-neutral application-occurrence contract over the existing occurrence relation, one reserved frontend-metadata key, and the already-ruled third trace field. Keep code content, provenance, attribution evidence, and verification results separate. Publish the durable correspondence as an additional addressable record referring to the oracle's evidence **without changing that evidence's format**. Production adoption includes Clojure, TypeScript, PHP, Python, their preludes, and the additional ingress paths inventoried below.\n45:Dedicated operations such as `:dao.stream.apply/call`, stream nodes, and continuation nodes retain existing instruction provenance. They are not silently counted as `:application` sites. Their adapters' application occurrences are covered; an extension to count other node kinds must explicitly version the coverage policy (`vm.cljc:1015-1028`; `semantic/linearize.cljc:126-139`).\n61:Implementation may add this marker to temporary frontend map nodes before side-table projection, since the current extractor recognizes namespace `yang` (`vm.cljc:1183-1214`). Prefer deriving the relation directly from validated rows where map attachment would be redundant. `ast->semantic-bytecode` hashes only grammar slots and drops other node keys (`vm.cljc:1113-1123`); no grammar slot, instruction operand, canonical datom slot, or AST contract revision is authorized by C1.\n69:Macro expansion already records input root, call path, macro root, parent, output and attempt (`src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:997-1000,1062-1119`). However, final tail marking and stand-in lowering happen later (`:1255-1260`). The implementation inventory must resolve how final published roots are related to these expansion outputs. Whether the existing event/record adapters already provide the complete final-root correspondence is **unknown — the implementation round's inventory decides**. Do not assume an intermediate output record identifies every occurrence in the final assembled tree, and do not mint a fake `:expand` merely to satisfy the shape.\n71:### 2.4 Lowering, reconstruction, and runtime lookup\n73:Persist the existing instruction relation `[segment-address pc origin root-address path]`. The row linearizer already returns `{:address ... :provenance [[pc origin root path] ...]}`; its caller supplies the segment coordinate (`semantic/linearize.cljc:188-214`; code-as-tuples §5.3 at lines 918–931). A call dispatch looks up its **own call instruction's** emitting occurrence, not the preceding operand's instruction or the callee's body. Multiple instructions may belong to one occurrence.\n75:Segment+PC alone is insufficient when identical code is loaded under two origins. The runtime must retain its load/admission origin alongside its image reference when selecting provenance. This is an occurrence-scoped runtime context, not a new code hash. The native image derivations must supply or independently reconstruct a mapping from their call PCs back through semantic PCs to K; PC equality across formats must not be assumed. Current semantic `resolve` preserves PCs, register derivation preserves layout, and stack derivation emits a new sequence (`semantic/derive.cljc:21-42,60-93,154-172`).\n79:### 2.5 Ephemeral origin and trace field\n116:| Code lowering profiles | Read, and verify unchanged code output. A separately versioned provenance recipe names any new side mapping. | Revise the relevant code profile if a pinned lowering rule or emitted code changes, even if execution grammar does not. A side-only addition must not masquerade as validation by the old derivation verifier. |\n121:| Continuation/UCF/checkpoint contracts | Read/inventory initially. | If a provenance-dependent consumer requires new serialized runtime state, inventory and version that actual state contract before shipping. A transient field cannot be silently serialized or relied on after migration. |\n124:Current constants: `src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:262-285`. Some design prose still says semantic `v3` (`docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:1879-1884`); implementation inventory must use the current source stamp, not copy stale prose. Revision principles: `code-as-tuples.md:859-913`, `yin.vm.linker.md:1871-1921`. Concrete closed validators: `linker.cljc:731-759,841-871`. Frontend revision semantics: `yang/frontend.cljc:15-16,87-98,166-200`.\n141:| Macro pipeline: `yin/vm/macro.cljc:997-1005,1062-1119,1196-1261` | Keep expansion attempt provenance, resolve final-root ownership, annotate generated occurrences and retain retrievable expansion records. Post-expansion is a required integration point. |\n142:| Safepoint insertion: `yang/safepoint.cljc:234-256` | Current derived envelope explicitly clears frontend metadata, source positions and entity occurrences. New root needs fresh C1 plus verified input/output mapping; preserve the safepoint record's separate semantics. |\n144:| Canonical and datom reconstruction: `yin/vm.cljc:705,818-851,1477-1555` | Side metadata does not reappear from content alone. Attach provenance context out of band; do not collapse occurrence ids via shared reconstruction/entity ids. Round-trip canonical hashes must remain equal. |\n145:| Semantic row and datom lowering: `yin/vm/semantic/linearize.cljc:106-128,188-221,283-302` | Persist returned provenance, supply origins explicitly, bridge datom entities with occurrences; nil-origin legacy calls remain loadable but cannot satisfy C1 attribution. |\n146:| Native lowering: `yin/vm/semantic/derive.cljc:21-42,60-120,154-184` | Produce/reconstruct complete native-call-PC provenance; compose with semantic mapping. Sparse variable/closure diagnostics do not establish call coverage. Inventory any legacy direct de Bruijn lowering still reached after merges. |\n147:| Runtime dispatch and resume: `yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:273-332,493,625-711,776`; `yin/vm/semantic.cljc:102,142,333-380` | Cold/hot application routes, operator/operand frames, linked image attachment and resumption all need occurrence context for traced execution. Native engines and UCF lifting must be audited too; no claim they already preserve caller K. |\n148:| Existing derivation verification: `yin/vm/ledger.cljc:156-215`, `yin/vm/linker.cljc:2164-2255` | Existing verification re-lowers code and compares identities, not side metadata. Keep code verification and add independent provenance/correspondence verification. `:trusted` does not satisfy this new acceptance gate. |\n149:| Generic publication: `yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:115-218` | Currently takes only `:vector` from `project-rows` at line 157. Publish complete side artifacts and origin/record closure beside code; do not discard provenance or change manifest keys casually. Validate before making a C1 availability claim. |\n152:| Code index / republishing: `yin/repl/ast_index.cljc:3-24,108`; `yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:225-300` | Query views can be rebuilt from rows but cannot recover omitted admission provenance. Session/index-to-module publication must preserve or explicitly remap occurrence roots, and retain original evidence sources. |\n153:| Oracle instrumentation/accounting: `test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:1786-1855,2341-2405,2432-2468` | Oracle-owned trace migration, offset retirement across the full family, both lanes, refusal pins, gate accounting and evidence regeneration per R5/R6. Audit table derivation independently per R7. |\n157:“Side tables are additive” is the compatibility policy, not permission to skip republishing coverage evidence. **Unannotated persisted artifacts remain loadable under their supported existing code contracts.** A provenance-requiring query returns missing provenance/refuses credit; it never invents identity from names, hashes, arities, values or source lines. Old provenance may be reconstructed only when the actual admission/expansion inputs are retained, and the result is a newly verified artifact.\n162:2. For each host publication: canonical row closure, semantic vector, stack/register stored images and checksums, derivation records, schema-1 manifest, pinned dependency addresses, new C1 side tables/provenance/correspondence, and discovery/index/name/signature records where used. The actual publication sequence is `yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:156-209`. Side-only annotation should leave old code addresses unchanged; measure this rather than assume it. New side objects still need publication, retrieval and retention.\n172:Preserve the existing failure categories: malformed canonical content fails structural validation; wrong bytes/address fails hash verification; unknown execution contract refuses load; differing lowering profile reports profile mismatch or `:unverified-derivation`; wrong recomputed output reports derivation mismatch (`vm.cljc:283-285`; `ledger.cljc:156-180`; `linker.cljc:2175-2215`). New evidence-specific failures must be distinguishable: unsupported C1/evidence version, missing provenance, bad occurrence, incomplete coverage, wrong source/adaptation hash, missing lane, ambiguous correspondence, stale declared site, or reconstruction mismatch.\n174:A corrupted required provenance artifact refuses the provenance-dependent operation even if code itself verifies. An absent optional annotation does not make old code unloadable. A signature authenticates a publisher; it does not verify a correspondence. A trusted code link is not evidence of a recomputed correspondence.\n196: :yang.correspondence/instruction-provenance\n197: [<Jing address of an instruction-provenance relation> ...]}\n200:The D1 key set is closed; vectors preserve declared order and contain no duplicate declarations. Empty artifact/provenance vectors are allowed for a tree-only consumer, whose claimed coverage must say tree-only. `artifacts` lists lowered images only; canonical roots are retrievable row closures listed separately. Native image identity need not equal its storage address: the publisher already distinguishes checksums and stored images (`publish.cljc:159-185,204-208`). The record's address is the ordinary Jing content address of the whole map; no self-address field.\n217:The recipe profile pins frontend/compiler revision, grammar/options, expansion environment and recorded origins/attempts, all code-producing transformations, source canonicalization, site-table derivation, and lowering/provenance rules. The consumer must implement that exact recipe/profile. A missing upstream canonicalization rule is not repaired by choosing a plausible hash. The exact oracle schema identity remains the dependency in §1.1.\n227:1. Fetch D1, recipe, OracleEvidence and every required source/row/provenance artifact; verify their addresses and structural schemas. Reject an unsupported oracle schema before interpreting any row.\n243:Each adoption brief must cite this findings file and C1/D1 by revision. The shared occurrence finalizer/validator and provenance publication work precede or accompany frontend implementation; D1 independent verification precedes acceptance of the first frontend consumer that relies on D1. A frontend may adopt static annotation without consuming oracle attribution, but cannot call that durable correspondence acceptance.\n258:On each host run static occurrence/provenance checks for AST, semantic, stack and register artifacts where the existing format admits the fixture. Execute direct walker and all three vector paths for the portable correctness corpus. Linked paths exercise all supported linked formats and pin existing unsupported/refusal cases explicitly. For example, the inspected TS linked harness documents a walker `:undeclared-free` limitation while using three vector VMs (`test/yang/typescript/antlr/linked_harness.cljc:11-15`); this design does not silently promise that old unrelated limitation is fixed. If a missing lane prevents the required new provenance test, report adoption incomplete with the named dependency.\n267:- Identical C1 rows, reconstructed site declarations, and per-format instruction provenance. Different formats have different PCs, but they must resolve to the same intended application K.\n284:Each brief's acceptance bundle contains: exact producer/artifact checklist; input/commit/profile pins; C1 coverage counts and root/hash invariance; four-host results and cross-host equality report; correctness battery results; before/after budgets and measurements; legacy-load/missing-provenance refusal; and D1 verification result if it relies on correspondence. No frontend can satisfy this bundle merely by running the oracle test.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
