8:   member claimed call-site (C1) coverage, holding one boolean verdict\n377:   call-site (C1) coverage is checked against its projected tree before\n378:   the batch is formed (`check-call-site-claim!`): the markers drop out\n400:         verdicts (mapv check-call-site-claim! asts row-sets)\n     1\t(ns yin.vm.encoder\n     2\t  \"The encoder observer: the `run-on-stream` consumer the composition\n     3\t   attaches over the program medium so the semantic VM never sees a map AST\n     4\t   or a datom batch (§9.1, `docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`).\n     5\t\n     6\t   It is a stage in its own right, not a loader arm. The composition hands\n     7\t   its session the row medium's writer as the consumer; `run-on-stream`\n     8\t   hands it each observed program batch; it projects the batch to the\n     9\t   canonical row set and forwards it — appends it to the row medium. The\n    10\t   evaluator observer the composition attaches to that row medium\n    11\t   independently is the next stage: the two never call each other, they\n    12\t   communicate through the row medium alone, so compiler and evaluator stay\n    13\t   independently attachable (no direct function-to-function coupling\n    14\t   without a stream boundary).\n    15\t\n    16\t   Projection is this boundary's one job (§9.1): the map AST the frontends\n    17\t   emit projects directly through `yin.vm/ast->semantic-bytecode`; a\n    18\t   `:yin/*` datom batch projects through the explicit datom→row adapter\n    19\t   below — §7.1's projection path, for media that carry datoms.\"\n    20\t  (:require [dao.stream :as stream]\n    21\t            [dao.stream.observer :as observer]\n    22\t            [yin.vm :as vm]))\n    23\t\n    24\t\n    25\t(defn source-envelope\n    26\t  \"Construct the provenance-bearing input value ruled by D4. The admitting\n    27\t   composition supplies its program medium's logical identity and mints a\n    28\t   fresh batch token before append. `members` is ordered and `root` selects\n    29\t   the member the evaluator runs. The constructor validates but never mints:\n    30\t   retries of an unaccepted staged append must retain the caller's token.\"\n    31\t  ([source-medium batch-token members]\n    32\t   (source-envelope source-medium batch-token members 0))\n    33\t  ([source-medium batch-token members root]\n    34\t   (let [envelope {:yin/source-medium source-medium\n    35\t                   :yin/batch-token batch-token\n    36\t                   :yin/batch (vec members)\n    37\t                   :yin/root root}]\n    38\t     ;; Validating the root origin validates the two opaque coordinates too.\n    39\t     (when-not (and (integer? root) (<= 0 root) (< root (count members)))\n    40\t       (throw (ex-info \"Malformed source batch envelope\"\n    41\t                       {:rule :batch-envelope, :envelope envelope})))\n    42\t     (vm/source-origin source-medium batch-token root)\n    43\t     envelope)))\n    44\t\n    45\t\n    46\t(defn- datom-child-places\n    47\t  [get-attr eid]\n    48\t  (case (get-attr eid :yin/type)\n    49\t    :lambda [[3 (get-attr eid :yin/body)]]\n    50\t    :application (concat [[2 (get-attr eid :yin/operator)]]\n    51\t                         (map-indexed (fn [i child] [[3 i] child])\n    52\t                                      (get-attr eid :yin/operands)))\n    53\t    :if [[2 (get-attr eid :yin/test)]\n    54\t         [3 (get-attr eid :yin/consequent)]\n    55\t         [4 (get-attr eid :yin/alternate)]]\n    56\t    :dao.stream.apply/call\n    57\t    (map-indexed (fn [i child] [[3 i] child])\n    58\t                 (get-attr eid :yin/operands))\n    59\t    :stream/put [[2 (get-attr eid :yin/target)]\n    60\t                 [3 (get-attr eid :yin/val-node)]]\n    61\t    (:stream/cursor :stream/next :stream/close)\n    62\t    [[2 (get-attr eid :yin/source)]]\n    63\t    :vm/resume [[3 (get-attr eid :yin/val-node)]]\n    64\t    []))\n    65\t\n    66\t\n    67\t(defn datom-entity-occurrences\n    68\t  \"The §9.1 datom adapter's path/entity relation for one admitted member:\n    69\t   `[member-index path entity-id]` once per structural occurrence. Shared\n    70\t   entities therefore appear at every path while retaining one entity\n    71\t   identity, which is the information U16's harvest catalogue derives from.\n    72\t   Cyclic or dangling legacy graphs fail at this boundary.\"\n    73\t  [datoms member-index]\n    74\t  (let [{:keys [root-id get-attr error]} (vm/index-datoms datoms)]\n    75\t    (when error\n    76\t      (throw (ex-info \"Cannot adapt a datom batch with a dangling root\" error)))\n    77\t    (when (nil? root-id)\n    78\t      (throw (ex-info \"Cannot adapt a datom batch with no root\"\n    79\t                      {:rule :root})))\n    80\t    (letfn [(walk\n    81\t              [eid path active]\n    82\t              (when (contains? active eid)\n    83\t                (throw (ex-info \"Cannot adapt a cyclic datom AST\"\n    84\t                                {:rule :cycle, :entity eid, :path path})))\n    85\t              (when (nil? (get-attr eid :yin/type))\n    86\t                (throw (ex-info \"Cannot adapt a dangling datom AST reference\"\n    87\t                                {:rule :dangling-ref, :entity eid, :path path})))\n    88\t              (into [[member-index path eid]]\n    89\t                    (mapcat (fn [[step child]]\n    90\t                              (walk child (conj path step) (conj active eid))))\n    91\t                    (datom-child-places get-attr eid)))]\n    92\t      (vec (walk root-id [] #{})))))\n    93\t\n    94\t\n    95\t(defn datoms->rows\n    96\t  \"§7.1's projection path as an explicit adapter: a `:yin/*` datom batch\n    97\t   reconstructs its map AST (`vm/datoms->ast`), then projects like every\n    98\t   other batch — the datom→row half of the §9.1 codec pair.\"\n    99\t  [datoms]\n   100\t  (vm/ast->semantic-bytecode (vm/datoms->ast datoms)))\n   101\t\n   102\t\n   103\t(declare check-projected-claim!)\n   104\t\n   105\t\n   106\t(defn- project-member\n   107\t  [member origin member-index]\n   108\t  (cond\n   109\t    (and (map? member) (contains? member :type))\n   110\t    (let [tree (vm/ast->semantic-bytecode member)]\n   111\t      (merge {:tree tree, :entity-occurrences []}\n   112\t             (vm/ast-side-tables member tree origin)))\n   113\t\n   114\t    (and (map? member) (contains? member :root) (contains? member :rows))\n   115\t    (let [metadata (:frontend-metadata member)]\n   116\t      (when (and (some? metadata) (not (sequential? metadata)))\n   117\t        (throw (ex-info \"Supplied frontend-metadata is not a row sequence\"\n   118\t                        {:rule :frontend-metadata, :member-index member-index,\n   119\t                         :metadata metadata})))\n   120\t      (check-projected-claim! metadata member)\n   121\t      {:tree member,\n   122\t       :source-positions (vec (or (:source-positions member) [])),\n   123\t       :frontend-metadata (vec (or metadata [])),\n   124\t       :entity-occurrences []})\n   125\t\n   126\t    (sequential? member)\n   127\t    (let [datoms (vec member)\n   128\t          ast (vm/datoms->ast datoms)\n   129\t          tree (vm/ast->semantic-bytecode ast)]\n   130\t      (merge {:tree tree\n   131\t              :entity-occurrences (datom-entity-occurrences datoms member-index)}\n   132\t             (vm/ast-side-tables ast tree origin)))\n   133\t\n   134\t    :else\n   135\t    (throw (ex-info \"Unsupported source batch member\"\n   136\t                    {:rule :batch-member, :member member,\n   137\t                     :member-index member-index}))))\n   138\t\n   139\t\n   140\t(defn- project-envelope\n   141\t  [{:yin/keys [source-medium batch-token batch root] :as envelope}]\n   142\t  (when-not (and (vector? batch)\n   143\t                 (seq batch)\n   144\t                 (integer? root)\n   145\t                 (<= 0 root)\n   146\t                 (< root (count batch)))\n   147\t    (throw (ex-info \"Malformed source batch envelope\"\n   148\t                    {:rule :batch-envelope, :envelope envelope})))\n   149\t  (let [projected (mapv (fn [j member]\n   150\t                          (project-member member\n   151\t                                          (vm/source-origin source-medium\n   152\t                                                            batch-token j)\n   153\t                                          j))\n   154\t                        (range) batch)]\n   155\t    (assoc envelope\n   156\t           :yin/batch (mapv :tree projected)\n   157\t           :yin/source-positions (into [] (mapcat :source-positions) projected)\n   158\t           :yin/frontend-metadata (into [] (mapcat :frontend-metadata) projected)\n   159\t           :yin/entity-occurrences (into [] (mapcat :entity-occurrences) projected))))\n   160\t\n   161\t\n   162\t(defn project\n   163\t  \"One program-medium batch to its canonical row set `{:root id, :rows …}`\n   164\t   (§6.1). A batch that is neither a map AST nor a datom batch is a defect\n   165\t   of the medium and throws here, at the boundary, naming where it failed.\"\n  2253\t                     ;; the intra-unit fast path: the callee names an\n  2254\t                     ;; instrumented definition — a direct site. The\n  2255\t                     ;; arguments are walked FIRST (their nested sites\n  2256\t                     ;; stamp during argument evaluation), then wrapped.\n  2257\t                     (and (= :application t) (kernel-intra-call? node)\n  2258\t                          (= :variable (:type (first (:operands node))))\n  2259\t                          (contains? instrumented (get-in node [:operands 0 :name])))\n  2260\t                     (let [callee (first (:operands node))\n  2261\t                           args-node (second (:operands node))\n  2262\t                           walked-args (walk args-node def-name scope params)]\n  2263\t                       (assoc node :operands [callee\n  2264\t                                              (stamp walked-args\n  2265\t                                                     (:name callee)\n  2266\t                                                     def-name scope params)]))\n  2267\t\n  2268\t                     ;; the admission-routed guest call: a computed or\n  2269\t                     ;; out-of-unit callee — an indirect site (R2). The\n  2270\t                     ;; stamp wraps the routed arguments binding; the walk\n  2271\t                     ;; then descends through the stamp into the argument\n  2272\t                     ;; expressions and the routing.\n  2273\t                     (and (= :application t) (kernel-routed-call? node))\n  2274\t                     (let [inner (kernel-routed-call? node)\n  2275\t                           stamped-inner (assoc inner :operands\n  2276\t                                                [(stamp (first (:operands inner))\n  2277\t                                                        :dynamic def-name scope params)])\n  2278\t                           walked-inner (walk stamped-inner def-name scope params)]\n  2279\t                       (-> node\n  2280\t                           (assoc-in [:operator :body] walked-inner)\n  2281\t                           (update :operands (fn [ops]\n  2282\t                                               (mapv #(walk % def-name scope params) ops)))))\n  2283\t\n  2284\t                     ;; let-lowering: a one-param lambda applied to one\n  2285\t                     ;; operand binds that parameter to the operand's\n  2286\t                     ;; expression for the site-shape resolution\n  2287\t                     (and (= :application t)\n  2288\t                          (= :lambda (:type (:operator node)))\n  2289\t                          (= 1 (count (:params (:operator node))))\n  2290\t                          (= 1 (count (:operands node))))\n  2291\t                     (let [[p init] [(first (:params (:operator node))) (first (:operands node))]\n  2292\t                           walked-init (walk init def-name scope params)]\n  2293\t                       (-> node\n  2294\t                           (assoc-in [:operator :body]\n  2295\t                                     (walk (:body (:operator node)) def-name\n  2296\t                                           (assoc scope p walked-init) params))\n  2297\t                           (assoc :operands [walked-init])))\n  2298\t\n  2299\t                     ;; an ordinary lambda's binders shadow: references to\n  2300\t                     ;; them are the caller-owned :variable shape\n  2301\t                     (and (map? node) (= :lambda t))\n  2302\t                     (assoc node :body (walk (:body node) def-name scope\n  2303\t                                             (into params (:params node))))\n  2304\t\n  2305\t                     :else (into {} (map (fn [[k v]]\n  2306\t                                           (if (coll? v)\n  2307\t                                             [k (walk v def-name scope params)]\n  2308\t                                             [k v])))\n  2309\t                                 node)))\n  2310\t                 (vector? node) (mapv #(walk % def-name scope params) node)\n  2311\t                 (seq? node) (apply list (map #(walk % def-name scope params) node))\n  2312\t                 :else node))\n  2313\t        [wrappers dispatcher-app main-body] (kernel-under-dispatcher ast)\n  2314\t        stamped-body (walk main-body nil {} #{})\n  2315\t        stamped (if dispatcher-app\n  2316\t                  (reduce (fn [body wrapper]\n  2317\t                            (assoc-in wrapper [:operator :body] body))\n  2318\t                          (assoc-in dispatcher-app [:operator :body] stamped-body)\n  2319\t                          (rseq wrappers))\n  2320\t                  stamped-body)]\n  2321\t    {:ast stamped :sites (vec @sites)}))\n  2322\t\n  2323\t\n  2324\t(defn- kernel-record-credits?\n  2325\t  \"One caller-site-tagged trace record `[fname arity caller-site]` against\n  2326\t   the attribution contract for upstream `symbol` at arity `n` (R1-R4):\n  2327\t   the degenerate 1:1 path matches the arity-preserving guest definition\n  2328\t   at any real site; a derived correspondence path matches its carrier\n  2329\t   callable and arity only through a SITE-LINKED record — the site\n  2330\t   table's static callee at that site is the carrier itself (R2b case 6:\n  2331\t   indirect origins refuse unless site-linked) — that is not a\n  2332\t   self-delegation (R2's rule: the originating call is the credited\n  2333\t   event). An :ambient-delegation path — the R3 disjointness pair —\n  2334\t   additionally requires the site's compiled context argument (position\n  2335\t   0) to have resolved to the adapter-supplied ambient literal;\n   195\t   (let [algo (clojure.core/get opts :algorithm default-hash-algorithm)]\n   196\t     (digest-bytes algo (canonical-bytes v)))))\n   197\t\n   198\t\n   199\t(defn segment-key\n   200\t  \"Mint the content-addressed key for an opaque payload, derived solely from\n   201\t   the payload: :segment/<algorithm-id>-<digest>.\n   202\t   The 1-arg form defaults to default-hash-algorithm (:blake3).\n   203\t   The 2-arg form accepts {:algorithm :sha256} (or any registered algorithm).\"\n   204\t  ([v]\n   205\t   (segment-key v {:algorithm default-hash-algorithm}))\n   206\t  ([v opts]\n   207\t   (let [algo (clojure.core/get opts :algorithm default-hash-algorithm)\n   208\t         reg (clojure.core/get registry algo)]\n   209\t     (when-not reg\n   210\t       (throw (ex-info (str \"unsupported hash algorithm: \" algo)\n   211\t                       {:algorithm algo})))\n   212\t     (let [algo-id (:address-id reg)\n   213\t           digest (content-hash v {:algorithm algo})]\n   214\t       (keyword \"segment\" (str algo-id \"-\" digest))))))\n   215\t\n   216\t\n   217\t(defn parse-segment-address\n   218\t  \"The authoritative address parser: requires a `segment` namespace keyword,\n   219\t   splits the name on the first `-` into algorithm identifier and digest,\n   220\t   looks the identifier up by exact match (no prefix matching), validates\n   385\t                           {:address address, :reason :non-canonical}))))\n   386\t    bs))\n   387\t\n   388\t\n   389\t(defn materialize!\n   390\t  \"Content-address payload and store it through the handle's byte store.\n   391\t\n   392\t   Supports [handle payload] (defaulting to default-hash-algorithm, :blake3)\n   393\t   and [handle payload {:keys [algorithm]}].\n   394\t\n   395\t   The payload is encoded exactly once. The address is derived solely from\n   396\t   those canonical bytes, and the same bytes go to the backend effect\n   397\t   :put-bytes-fn as (put-bytes-fn address bytes). The backend must answer\n   398\t   with one of:\n   399\t\n   400\t     :inserted -- the bytes are durably stored now;\n   401\t     :present  -- bytes are already stored under that address.\n   402\t\n   403\t   Any other answer is an invalid backend result and throws: DaoJing does\n   404\t   not accept ambiguous truthiness. The address is returned only after the\n   405\t   backend reports success.\n   406\t\n   407\t   On :present the stored bytes are read back through :get-bytes-fn and\n   408\t   verified both by hash against the address and byte for byte against the\n   409\t   proposed bytes. Equal content is idempotent and returns the same\n   410\t   address. Content that does not hash to its own address is an integrity\n   411\t   failure and throws loudly. Nothing is ever overwritten.\"\n   412\t  ([handle payload]\n   413\t   (materialize! handle payload {:algorithm default-hash-algorithm}))\n   414\t  ([handle payload opt