pace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794357400000-engineer-cc3-resume.glm-5.3.stdout.log\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794343683000-architect-cc3-d1-mob.deepseek-v4-pro.prompt.md\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794343683000-architect-cc3-d1-mob.gemini-4-argon-high.prompt.md\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794357169000-engineer-cc3-lowering-provenance.prompt.md\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794343683000-architect-cc3-d1-mob.deepseek-v4-pro.stdout.log\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc3/collab/yang-clojure/1794343683000-architect-cc3-d1-mob.deepseek-v4-pro.stdout.log\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc3/collab/yang-clojure/1794343683000-architect-cc3-d1-mob.deepseek-v4-pro.prompt.md\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc3/collab/yang-clojure/1794343683000-architect-cc3-d1-mob.gemini-4-argon-high.prompt.md\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc3/collab/yang-clojure/1794343683000-architect-cc3-d1-mob.gemini-4-argon-high.findings.md\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc3/collab/yang-clojure/1794343683000-architect-cc3-d1-mob.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc3/collab/yang-clojure/1794343683000-architect-cc3-d1-mob.gpt-6-astra.findings.md\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc3/collab/yang-clojure/1794343683000-architect-cc3-d1-mob.gpt-6-astra.prompt.md\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc3/collab/yang-clojure/1794343683000-architect-cc3-d1-mob.prompt.md\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc3/collab/yang-clojure/1794343683000-architect-cc3-d1-mob.gemini-4-argon-high.stdout.log\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc3/collab/yang-clojure/1794357169000-engineer-cc3-lowering-provenance.muse-wonder-1.0.findings.md\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc3/collab/yang-clojure/1794344088000-orchestrator-cc3-d1-mob-reconciliation.md\n    22\tThe inspected worktree `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-aaq`, branch `attribution-as-query`, reports HEAD `3eebe844e406ba56bc38a5f4b4a0098d811b5efd`. Its tracked `test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:1794-1839` still generates two-field body-entry traces and uses the offset accounting at lines 1786–1819. Its `BRIEF-aaq.md:15-21` requires the future inventory, three-field instrumentation, and versioned mechanically derived site tables; it does not define their schema. Untracked `probe/aaq_probe.clj:17-29` uses exploratory map-key paths, not the canonical row-position paths; it is not a published contract. The local dispatch log `collab/yang-clojure/1794630000000-engineer-attribution-as-query.glm-5.3.stdout.log:1` reports an unrecognized-model error. This does not establish the status of work elsewhere.\n    23\t\n    24\tConsequently **the oracle evidence schema's exact identifier, field names, and canonical-source-hash algorithm/profile are unknown — the implementation round's inventory decides from the oracle's accepted record**. This document specifies the envelope around that record and its verification obligations, not an independently invented replacement site-table schema. “OracleEvidence” below means the entire accepted versioned oracle value, verbatim, including its site ids, argument-shape declarations, fixture linkage, trace schema, and version. Before durable implementation is accepted, the inventory must pin its concrete file, commit, schema identifier/version, validator, and derivation entry point. Absence of that pin blocks durable acceptance; it does not block the oracle round.\n    25\t\n    26\tThis is the format-race control demanded by A4. An implementation must not adopt a hypothetical table in this document or mistake `throw_sites.edn` for the new caller-site table.\n    27\t\n    28\t## 2. Occurrence contract C1\n    29\t\n    30\tThe following is the proposed normative contract, grounded in `docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:393-455,658-665,918-931`.\n    31\t\n    32\t### 2.1 Identity and scope\n    33\t\n    34\tAn application site is `K = [origin root-address path]`.\n    35\t\n    36\t- `root-address` is the canonical root of the particular final UAST tree being named, not the application's subtree address or an instrumented runtime-map hash.\n    37\t- `path` is the grammar's structural path: scalar child slot steps are row positions; a child in a `nodes` slot is `[slot-position member-index]`. The root is `[]`. Never substitute map keys, host iteration order, allocation ids, source line numbers, PCs, callee names, or arities.\n    38\t- `origin` identifies the actual admission or generation event. Admitted syntax uses `[:source medium batch-token j]`; generated syntax uses `[:expansion expansion-record-address]`. Generated post-expansion trees must not be mislabeled as original source or given a fabricated expansion address. If a no-op expansion retains an admitted tree, the original source origin remains valid; “post-expansion” names the pipeline boundary, not proof that a generating event occurred.\n    39\t- An origin is supplied by the admitting/generating composition. Two separately admitted identical sources have different origins; two identical applications in one tree have different paths; two expansion attempts with identical output have different producing records. Replaying a single recorded admission is different from admitting the same text again.\n    40\t\n    41\tThe implemented origin validator accepts exactly the source and expansion shapes (`src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:938-958`). `vm/occurrences` returns `[root path node-id]`, one per place, with origin fixed by the composition; it does not return full keys or count only distinct row ids (`vm.cljc:1589-1609`).\n    42\t\n    71\t### 2.4 Lowering, reconstruction, and runtime lookup\n    72\t\n    73\tPersist the existing instruction relation `[segment-address pc origin root-address path]`. The row linearizer already returns `{:address ... :provenance [[pc origin root path] ...]}`; its caller supplies the segment coordinate (`semantic/linearize.cljc:188-214`; code-as-tuples §5.3 at lines 918–931). A call dispatch looks up its **own call instruction's** emitting occurrence, not the preceding operand's instruction or the callee's body. Multiple instructions may belong to one occurrence.\n    74\t\n    75\tSegment+PC alone is insufficient when identical code is loaded under two origins. The runtime must retain its load/admission origin alongside its image reference when selecting provenance. This is an occurrence-scoped runtime context, not a new code hash. The native image derivations must supply or independently reconstruct a mapping from their call PCs back through semantic PCs to K; PC equality across formats must not be assumed. Current semantic `resolve` preserves PCs, register derivation preserves layout, and stack derivation emits a new sequence (`semantic/derive.cljc:21-42,60-93,154-172`).\n    76\t\n    77\tCanonical reconstruction deliberately shares nodes by content (`vm.cljc:1477-1486,1522-1525`). Never cache an occurrence annotation by node-id or mutate a shared canonical reconstruction and expect distinct sites to survive. Keep an occurrence cursor/side lookup, or expand occurrence-specific ephemeral images. The datom linearizer currently returns `[pc entity]`, not K (`semantic/linearize.cljc:283-302`); use an explicit entity-to-occurrence bridge with the owning batch, root and path, refusing ambiguity. `encoder/datom-entity-occurrences` and the envelope's `:yin/entity-occurrences` are the starting machinery (`encoder.cljc:67,115-148`).\n    78\t\n    79\t### 2.5 Ephemeral origin and trace field\n    80\t\n   225\tVerification proceeds in this order:\n   226\t\n   227\t1. Fetch D1, recipe, OracleEvidence and every required source/row/provenance artifact; verify their addresses and structural schemas. Reject an unsupported oracle schema before interpreting any row.\n   228\t2. Verify each code image under its own execution contract and its existing derivation chain. Report content integrity separately from code derivation.\n   229\t3. Reconstruct the source/adaptation compilation under the exact recipe, using recorded source admissions and expansion attempts; recompute final roots and eligible occurrence sets. Recompute the oracle's canonical-source-hash using its exact rule.\n   230\t4. Independently re-derive the oracle site declarations from compiled AST argument expressions. Compare with OracleEvidence verbatim under that schema's comparison rules. Do not make the producer's serialized classifications the verifier's input oracle.\n   231\t5. Reconstruct every claimed ephemeral-to-persisted binding, C1 marker set and instruction mapping. Verify exact coverage and all multiplicities, including helpers that receive no upstream credit.\n   232\t6. Execute the attribution query over separately scoped evidence/occurrence/row relations; require actual body entry in both required oracle C lanes. Reconstruct the credit set and report withdrawn witnesses and stale declarations. R7 requires re-derivation, not just re-running a query over trusted declarations (`ruling-of-record.md:48-50`).\n   233\t7. Publish a separate verification result naming D1's address, recipe/profile, verifier revision, inputs, outcomes and evidence. D1 cannot mark itself “verified”. The first additional consumer's acceptance must cite this completed result.\n   234\t\n   235\tAn implementation may share the schema decoder and canonical primitives, but a verifier must not call the producer's table derivation as its only “independent” check. The existing table generator resolves the oracle's own private derivation vars (`latte_oracle_tables.clj:15-18`); regeneration through it alone does not satisfy R7.\n   236\t\n   237\tD1 does not revise old site tables, force old module manifests to schema 2, or automatically validate the oracle's historical evidence. If actual integration requires changing a closed publication record, that specific schema revision returns to the A2 inventory. Unknown recipes refuse verification; `:trusted` is never substituted for `:verifying` (linker design `:1902-1921`).\n   238\t\n   239\t## 5. A3 adoption briefs and ordering\n   240\t\n   241\t**Scheduling:** frontend adoption starts after the in-flight cell-ref sweeps and the J2 re-run land. Refresh this inventory on that merged base before changing shared prelude/publication paths. The current sweeps, J2 and oracle continue under their existing scopes. This design does not merge, pause, or extend their tasks (signed A3/A4 at lines 21–25).\n   242\t\n   920\t   continuation, a host type only a kernel mints, counts as its payload,\n   921\t   as the plain map it was before D7. Rows and code datoms stay\n   922\t   `plain-data?`, plus the exact-integer big carriers (ruling 4).\"\n   923\t  [x]\n   924\t  (or (nil? x)\n   925\t      (if (values/host-typed? x)\n   926\t        (machine-data? (values/payload x))\n   927\t        (and (cond (or (boolean? x) (number? x)\n   928\t                       (integer-host/big-carrier? x) (cbor/float64? x)\n   929\t                       (string? x) (keyword? x) (symbol? x))\n   930\t                   true\n   931\t                   (map? x) (and (every? machine-data? (keys x))\n   932\t                                 (every? machine-data? (vals x)))\n   933\t                   (coll? x) (every? machine-data? x)\n   934\t                   :else false)\n   935\t             (machine-data? (meta x))))))\n   936\t\n   937\t\n   938\t(defn occurrence-origin?\n   939\t  \"True when `origin` has one of §2.5's portable ruled shapes. `nil` is not\n   940\t   an occurrence; it is accepted separately by the legacy bare-row lane.\n   941\t\n   942\t   A source origin is `[:source medium batch-token member-index]`. Medium and\n   943\t   token are opaque non-nil plain data supplied by the admitting composition,\n   944\t   and the member index is a non-negative integer. An expansion origin names\n   945\t   the portable content address of its expansion record.\"\n   946\t  [origin]\n   947\t  (and (vector? origin)\n   948\t       (case (first origin)\n   949\t         :source (and (= 4 (count origin))\n   950\t                      (some? (nth origin 1))\n   951\t                      (plain-data? (nth origin 1))\n   952\t                      (some? (nth origin 2))\n   953\t                      (plain-data? (nth origin 2))\n   954\t                      (integer? (nth origin 3))\n   955\t                      (not (neg? (nth origin 3))))\n   956\t         :expansion (and (= 2 (count origin))\n   957\t                         (jing/segment-address? (nth origin 1)))\n   958\t         false)))\n   959\t\n   960\t\n   961\t(defn source-origin\n   962\t  \"Construct and validate the source occurrence coordinate owned by a batch\n   963\t   envelope. Throws with `:rule :origin` instead of allowing an incomplete\n   964\t   coordinate to enter a provenance or frontend side table.\"\n   965\t  [medium batch-token member-index]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
